1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,770
A single tank, and whole companies pulled back.

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:11,250
The Tiger I first reached the front near Leningrad in August 1942.

3
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:17,140
Its 8.8 cm gun knocked out enemy tanks at more than a kilometre,

4
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:20,670
and its front armour was ten centimetres thick.

5
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:23,930
Many Allied shells simply bounced off.

6
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:26,300
But the Tiger came at a price.

7
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:32,560
It weighed 57 tonnes, burned several hundred litres of petrol per hundred kilometres

8
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:35,550
and often broke down.

9
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:38,450
Many Tigers were not lost in battle,

10
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:42,630
they broke down and were blown up by their own crews.

11
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:46,570
Only about 1,350 were built.

12
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:51,150
In the same period the Soviet Union built tens of thousands of T-34s.

13
00:00:51,860 --> 00:00:53,970
The Tiger was feared,

14
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:58,550
but it came too late and in too small numbers to turn the war.
